# Netlify redirect table for URLs from the pre-migration site.
#
# The old site served posts under `/blog/<slug>/` (see the legacy RSS) and had a
# `/blog` index. Those URLs are live and indexed, so they must not 404 once this
# build takes over the domain. Netlify serves this file from the publish root
# and applies it at the edge as real 301s; `astro.config.mjs` declares the same
# pair for hosts that ignore `_redirects` (and for `astro dev` / `astro preview`).

# The trailing `!` is required. Astro's static build also emits an HTML
# meta-refresh page for each configured redirect (build.redirects defaults to
# true), and Netlify serves an existing static file in preference to a
# `_redirects` rule — "shadowing". Forcing the rule makes the real 301 win at
# the edge; the emitted HTML stays as the fallback for hosts that ignore
# `_redirects`, and for `astro dev` / `astro preview`.
#
# Netlify's own pretty-URL 301 (`/x` -> `/x/`) runs first, so the no-slash
# variants below only ever see already-normalized input.
#
# The legacy blog index has no counterpart here; the archive is its nearest
# equivalent. Listed before the splat rules so `/blog` is not swallowed by them.
/blog     /archive/   301!
/blog/    /archive/   301!

# Post permalinks: /blog/<slug>/ -> /posts/<slug>/ (all 15 legacy slugs exist
# under /posts/ after the content migration, so the mapping is a complete 1:1).
# The trailing-slash form is listed first so a slashed legacy URL resolves in
# a single hop.
/blog/:slug/   /posts/:slug/   301!
/blog/:slug    /posts/:slug    301!

# Fallback for any remaining legacy path under /blog (e.g. the exported JSON
# payloads that referenced /blog/<slug> without a trailing slash).
/blog/*    /posts/:splat   301!

# NOT redirected (deliberate, see the t9 report):
#   /categories/<name>/ and /tags/<name>/ are live legacy URLs that will 404
#   after cutover. Arbitrary percent-encoded CJK names and a case-folding tag
#   space (the live site lowercases /tags/CSS/ -> /tags/css/) do not map onto a
#   static redirect table; this site instead routes taxonomy to
#   /archive/?category=... via getCategoryUrl() in src/utils/url-utils.ts.
